.TH CBMAP_INIT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init \- create an ordered map with defaults into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init(hm_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map with the default allocator and the default order for
the declared key type, and assigns that map to hm_name. The caller must
declare the handle first, with cbmap_declare(3) or cbmap_declare_scoped(3).
The key type and the value type do not come to this macro as arguments: the
macro reads them from the companion type variables that the declare macro
created.

This is the narrowest of the init macros. cbmap_init_mp(3) adds an
allocator, cbmap_init_cc(3) adds a comparison procedure, and
cbmap_init_full(3) takes both.

The one-step equivalents are cbmap_construct(3) and
cbmap_construct_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  The map handle that the caller declared before, into which
         the macro creates the map
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "ORDERING"
.nf
The container is an AVL tree, so it keeps its elements in key order and
the walk visits them in ascending key order, as cbmap_begin_iter(3)
describes. The order comes from the declared key type: the signed integer
types get a signed integer comparison, the unsigned types and pointers get
an unsigned comparison, the floating-point types get a numeric comparison,
and a string key gets a lexicographic byte comparison.
cbmap_create_full(3) gives all of these in full, and also says where a NaN
key sorts.

A key type that the map does not recognize, such as a struct, gets a raw
comparison of the representation of the key, which reads padding bytes and
orders a pointer member by its address. Use cbmap_init_cc(3) for such a
key type.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro uses the default allocator
Only the key type reaches the container; only the type-inferred macros read
the declared value type
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare(sorted, int, char *);
cbmap_init(sorted);
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
k = 10;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "world");    /* The walk visits 10 before 42 */
cbmap_destroy(sorted);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
